  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-334460",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=t\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3t\n$$\n\neach of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ r(3t)={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ 3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtract the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{{e}^{3t}-{e}^{t}}{2t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{3{e}^{t}-{e}^{3t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the diagonal entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{{e}^{3t}-{e}^{t}}{2t}\\cdot t+\\frac{3{e}^{t}-{e}^{3t}}{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{t} \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =3\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{3t}-{e}^{t}}{2t}\\cdot t+\\frac{3{e}^{t}-{e}^{3t}}{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
